<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<section xml:id="sec-standard-deviation">
  <title>The Standard Deviation</title>

  <objectives xml:id="objectives-standard-deviation">
    <ul>
      <li>
        <p>Calculate the standard deviation of a small data set.</p>
      </li>
      <li>
        <p>Explain how squaring the deviations differs from taking their
        absolute values.</p>
      </li>
      <li>
        <p>Identify each symbol in the formula for the standard
        deviation.</p>
      </li>
      <li>
        <p>Use an interval one standard deviation on each side of the
        mean to describe a typical range of values.</p>
      </li>
      <li>
        <p>Compare the variability of two distributions using their
        standard deviations.</p>
      </li>
      <li>
        <p>Explain why the mean and the standard deviation carry
        independent information about a distribution.</p>
      </li>
    </ul>
  </objectives>

  <introduction>
    <p>
      In <xref ref="sec-average-deviation"/> we rescued the average of
      the deviations by taking absolute values first. That repair works,
      and the number it produces is honest: the ADM really is the
      average distance from the mean. Yet almost no statistician reports
      it.
    </p>
    <p>
      What they report instead is the <term>standard deviation</term>,
      which comes from making a different repair to the same problem.
      Everything else about the construction is identical. We will build
      it on the same seven numbers, so that the only thing that changes
      is the one step in the middle.
    </p>
  </introduction>

  <subsection xml:id="subsec-from-adm-to-sd">
    <title>Squaring instead of taking absolute values</title>

    <p>
      Recall the problem. The deviations <m>x - \bar{x}</m> add to zero
      for every data set, so averaging them directly is useless. We need
      some way to strip off the signs before adding.
    </p>

    <p>
      Absolute value is one way. Squaring is another: a square is never
      negative either. Returning to the sample 2, 2, 4, 5, 6, 7, 9 with
      mean 5, whose deviations we tabulated in
      <xref ref="table-adm-deviations"/>, we now square each one instead
      of taking its absolute value.
    </p>

    <table xml:id="table-sd-squared-deviations">
      <title>Squaring the deviations rather than taking absolute
      values</title>
      <tabular halign="center">
        <row header="yes" bottom="minor">
          <cell>Value <m>x</m></cell>
          <cell><m>x - \bar{x}</m></cell>
          <cell><m>|x - \bar{x}|</m></cell>
          <cell><m>(x - \bar{x})^2</m></cell>
        </row>
        <row><cell>2</cell><cell><m>-3</m></cell><cell>3</cell><cell>9</cell></row>
        <row><cell>2</cell><cell><m>-3</m></cell><cell>3</cell><cell>9</cell></row>
        <row><cell>4</cell><cell><m>-1</m></cell><cell>1</cell><cell>1</cell></row>
        <row><cell>5</cell><cell><m>0</m></cell><cell>0</cell><cell>0</cell></row>
        <row><cell>6</cell><cell><m>+1</m></cell><cell>1</cell><cell>1</cell></row>
        <row><cell>7</cell><cell><m>+2</m></cell><cell>2</cell><cell>4</cell></row>
        <row bottom="minor">
          <cell>9</cell><cell><m>+4</m></cell><cell>4</cell><cell>16</cell>
        </row>
        <row><cell>Total</cell><cell><m>0</m></cell><cell>14</cell><cell>40</cell></row>
      </tabular>
    </table>

    <p>
      The two repairs are not interchangeable. Taking absolute values
      treats every unit of distance alike: moving a value one unit
      farther from the mean adds exactly 1 to the total, wherever it
      starts. Squaring does not. The value 9 sits four units from the
      mean, twice as far as the 7 sits at two units, but it contributes
      16 to the total against the 7's 4 <mdash/> four times as much, not
      twice.
    </p>

    <figure xml:id="fig-sd-squares">
      <caption>How much each value contributes to the total of 40. The
      single value farthest from the mean supplies 16 of it.</caption>
      <image xml:id="image-sd-squares" source="R-generated/sd-squares"
             width="80%">
        <description>
          A bar chart with one bar for each of the seven data values 2,
          2, 4, 5, 6, 7, and 9, where the height of each bar is that
          value's squared deviation from the mean of 5. The bars are 9,
          9, 1, 0, 1, 4, and 16. The bar above the value 5 has height
          zero. The bar above 9 is shaded differently and is by far the
          tallest at 16, taller than the two bars of height 9 above the
          2s. The heights total 40, so the single value 9 accounts for
          nearly two fifths of the total by itself.
        </description>
      </image>
    </figure>

    <p>
      This is a feature, not an accident. Squaring makes the measure pay
      disproportionate attention to values far from the center, which is
      often exactly where the interesting behavior of a distribution
      lies. It also makes the resulting quantity far easier to work with
      algebraically than an absolute value, which is the deeper reason
      statistics was built on it. The cost is that the measure is
      sensitive to extreme values <mdash/> a point we return to below.
    </p>

    <exercise xml:id="check-sd-why-square">
      <statement>
        <p>
          Why does squaring the deviations solve the same problem that
          taking absolute values solved?
        </p>
      </statement>
      <choices randomize="yes">
        <choice correct="yes">
          <statement>
            <p>A square is never negative, so the deviations no longer
            cancel when added.</p>
          </statement>
          <feedback>
            <p>
              Correct. The cancellation came entirely from the signs.
              Any operation that removes them without discarding the
              distances will do, and squaring is one such operation.
            </p>
          </feedback>
        </choice>
        <choice>
          <statement>
            <p>Squaring makes all the deviations equal to each
            other.</p>
          </statement>
          <feedback>
            <p>
              It does not. In this sample the squared deviations are 9,
              9, 1, 0, 1, 4, and 16, which are far from equal. Squaring
              removes the signs while preserving, and in fact
              exaggerating, the differences in size.
            </p>
          </feedback>
        </choice>
        <choice>
          <statement>
            <p>Squaring converts the deviations back into the original
            data values.</p>
          </statement>
          <feedback>
            <p>
              A squared deviation is not a data value. Squaring the
              deviation of the 9, which is <m>+4</m>, gives 16, which
              has nothing to do with the number 9.
            </p>
          </feedback>
        </choice>
        <choice>
          <statement>
            <p>Squaring guarantees the total will be smaller than the
            total of the absolute values.</p>
          </statement>
          <feedback>
            <p>
              The opposite happened here: the absolute deviations total
              14 and the squared deviations total 40. Squaring usually
              inflates the total, because most deviations larger than 1
              grow when squared.
            </p>
          </feedback>
        </choice>
      </choices>
    </exercise>

    <exercise xml:id="check-sd-squared-deviation">
      <statement>
        <p>
          A data set has mean 30. One of its values is 36. What does
          this value contribute to the total of the squared deviations?
        </p>
      </statement>
      <choices randomize="yes">
        <choice correct="yes">
          <statement><p>36</p></statement>
          <feedback>
            <p>
              Correct. The deviation is <m>36 - 30 = 6</m>, and
              <m>6^2 = 36</m>. That the answer coincides with the data
              value here is a coincidence of the numbers chosen.
            </p>
          </feedback>
        </choice>
        <choice>
          <statement><p>6</p></statement>
          <feedback>
            <p>
              That is the deviation itself, which is what this value
              would contribute to the ADM calculation. The standard
              deviation squares it first.
            </p>
          </feedback>
        </choice>
        <choice>
          <statement><p>1296</p></statement>
          <feedback>
            <p>
              This squares the data value rather than its deviation.
              Compute the deviation first, then square.
            </p>
          </feedback>
        </choice>
        <choice>
          <statement><p>900</p></statement>
          <feedback>
            <p>
              This squares the mean. The quantity being squared is the
              distance from the mean, not the mean itself.
            </p>
          </feedback>
        </choice>
      </choices>
    </exercise>
  </subsection>

  <subsection xml:id="subsec-sd-formula">
    <title>From the total to the standard deviation</title>

    <p>
      We have a total of 40. Two steps remain, and each repairs a
      problem the previous step created.
    </p>

    <p>
      First, average. For the ADM we divided by <m>n</m>. Here we divide
      by <m>n - 1</m>, which for our seven values means dividing by 6:
    </p>

    <md>
      <mrow>\frac{40}{6} \approx 6.67.</mrow>
    </md>

    <p>
      The divisor <m>n - 1</m> rather than <m>n</m> is a small
      correction. A sample tends to sit slightly closer to its own mean
      than to the mean of the population it came from, so dividing by
      <m>n</m> would systematically understate the spread. Shrinking the
      divisor by one compensates. For large samples the adjustment
      barely matters; for small ones it matters a great deal. This
      quantity has its own name, the <term>variance</term>, written
      <m>s^2</m>.
    </p>

    <p>
      Second, undo the squaring. The variance is in squared units
      <mdash/> if the data were pounds, the variance is in square
      pounds, which describes nothing anyone can picture. Taking the
      square root returns us to the units of the data:
    </p>

    <md>
      <mrow>s = \sqrt{6.67} \approx 2.58.</mrow>
    </md>

    <p>
      This is the <term>standard deviation</term> of the sample, written
      <m>s</m>. Assembling the three steps gives the formula:
    </p>

    <md>
      <mrow>s = \sqrt{\frac{\sum (x - \bar{x})^2}{n - 1}}.</mrow>
    </md>

    <p>
      Reading it from the inside out: <m>x - \bar{x}</m> is a deviation
      from the mean; squaring it removes the sign; <m>\sum</m> adds the
      squares over all the data; dividing by <m>n - 1</m> averages them;
      and the square root returns the result to the original units. Here
      <m>n</m> is the number of observations and <m>\bar{x}</m> is the
      sample mean.
    </p>

    <p>
      Three properties follow directly from the construction, and they
      mirror the ones we noted for the ADM. The standard deviation
      carries the same units as the data. It can never be negative,
      since it is a square root of a sum of squares. And it equals zero
      exactly when every value is identical, which is the only way for
      every squared deviation to vanish.
    </p>

    <example xml:id="ex-sd-quiz">
      <title>Computing a standard deviation</title>
      <statement>
        <p>
          Four quiz scores are 6, 7, 11, and 16. Find the standard
          deviation, and compare it with the ADM of 3.5 computed for
          these same scores in <xref ref="sec-average-deviation"/>.
        </p>
      </statement>
      <solution>
        <p>
          The mean is <m>40/4 = 10</m>, so the deviations are <m>-4</m>,
          <m>-3</m>, <m>+1</m>, and <m>+6</m>. Squaring gives 16, 9, 1,
          and 36, which total 62. With <m>n = 4</m> we divide by 3:
        </p>
        <md>
          <mrow>s = \sqrt{\frac{62}{3}} = \sqrt{20.67} \approx
            4.55.</mrow>
        </md>
        <p>
          The standard deviation of 4.55 is noticeably larger than the
          ADM of 3.5 on identical data. The score of 16 is responsible:
          it sits 6 points from the mean, and squaring turns that into
          36, more than half of the total of 62. The ADM counts it as
          just 6 of 14.
        </p>
      </solution>
    </example>

    <exercise xml:id="check-sd-compute">
      <statement>
        <p>
          A sample of 10 values has squared deviations totaling 90. What
          is the standard deviation?
        </p>
      </statement>
      <choices randomize="yes">
        <choice correct="yes">
          <statement><p>About 3.16</p></statement>
          <feedback>
            <p>
              Correct. Divide by <m>n - 1 = 9</m> to get a variance of
              10, then take the square root:
              <m>\sqrt{10} \approx 3.16</m>.
            </p>
          </feedback>
        </choice>
        <choice>
          <statement><p>10</p></statement>
          <feedback>
            <p>
              That is the variance, <m>90/9</m>. One step remains: take
              the square root, so the answer returns to the units of the
              data.
            </p>
          </feedback>
        </choice>
        <choice>
          <statement><p>3</p></statement>
          <feedback>
            <p>
              This divides by <m>n = 10</m> rather than by
              <m>n - 1 = 9</m>. The smaller divisor is deliberate, and
              it makes the standard deviation slightly larger.
            </p>
          </feedback>
        </choice>
        <choice>
          <statement><p>9</p></statement>
          <feedback>
            <p>
              This is <m>n - 1</m>, the divisor itself rather than the
              result of dividing by it.
            </p>
          </feedback>
        </choice>
      </choices>
    </exercise>

    <exercise xml:id="check-sd-units">
      <statement>
        <p>
          The weights of a sample of packages are measured in pounds and
          have a standard deviation of 4. Which statement is
          correct?
        </p>
      </statement>
      <choices randomize="yes">
        <choice correct="yes">
          <statement>
            <p>The standard deviation is 4 pounds, and the variance is
            16 square pounds.</p>
          </statement>
          <feedback>
            <p>
              Correct. The square root at the end of the calculation is
              what returns the standard deviation to the units of the
              data. The variance, <m>s^2</m>, remains in squared units,
              which is why it is rarely reported on its own.
            </p>
          </feedback>
        </choice>
        <choice>
          <statement>
            <p>Both the standard deviation and the variance are measured
            in pounds.</p>
          </statement>
          <feedback>
            <p>
              The variance is an average of squared deviations, so its
              units are squared too. Only after the square root do we
              return to pounds.
            </p>
          </feedback>
        </choice>
        <choice>
          <statement>
            <p>The standard deviation has no units, since it is a
            measure of spread rather than a measurement.</p>
          </statement>
          <feedback>
            <p>
              It does have units. A standard deviation of 4 pounds and
              one of 4 ounces describe very different distributions,
              which is precisely why the units must be stated.
            </p>
          </feedback>
        </choice>
        <choice>
          <statement>
            <p>The standard deviation could be negative if most packages
            weigh less than the mean.</p>
          </statement>
          <feedback>
            <p>
              It could not. Every squared deviation is zero or positive,
              so their sum is too, and a square root of a nonnegative
              number is never negative.
            </p>
          </feedback>
        </choice>
      </choices>
    </exercise>
  </subsection>

  <subsection xml:id="subsec-sd-band">
    <title>A typical range of values about the mean</title>

    <p>
      The standard deviation is read the same way the ADM was. Mark the
      mean, then mark one standard deviation on each side of it. For our
      sample that band runs from <m>5 - 2.58 = 2.42</m> to
      <m>5 + 2.58 = 7.58</m>.
    </p>

    <figure xml:id="fig-sd-band">
      <caption>The interval one standard deviation on each side of the
      mean. Compare its width with the ADM band in
      <xref ref="fig-adm-band"/>.</caption>
      <image xml:id="image-sd-band" source="R-generated/sd-band"
             width="80%">
        <description>
          The dotplot of 2, 2, 4, 5, 6, 7, 9 with a shaded horizontal
          band running from about 2.42 to about 7.58, centered on the
          dashed mean line at 5. The values 4, 5, 6, and 7 fall inside
          the band, while the two dots at 2 and the dot at 9 lie
          outside it. The band is wider on each side than the average
          deviation band from 3 to 7 shown earlier, but it happens to
          contain the same four values.
        </description>
      </image>
    </figure>

    <p>
      Interpret it exactly as before: a typical observation sits about
      2.58 units from the mean, and most of the data lie within that
      distance of the center. On a graph, the practical skill is the
      same one as for the ADM. Find the mean by eye, then judge how wide
      an interval around it is needed to capture the bulk of the data;
      half that width estimates the standard deviation.
    </p>

    <p>
      Notice what this comparison shows. The ADM of this sample is 2 and
      the standard deviation is 2.58, so the SD band is wider. Both
      bands happen to catch the same four of the seven values here, but
      the standard deviation reports a larger typical distance, because
      squaring gave the 9 and the two 2s more influence than averaging
      their raw distances did. On almost any data set the standard
      deviation comes out at least as large as the ADM, and the gap
      widens as the distribution grows more extreme in its tails.
    </p>

    <exercise xml:id="check-sd-band-meaning">
      <statement>
        <p>
          A sample has mean 100 and standard deviation 12. Which
          interval is the typical range of values about the mean?
        </p>
      </statement>
      <choices randomize="yes">
        <choice correct="yes">
          <statement><p>From 88 to 112</p></statement>
          <feedback>
            <p>
              Correct. The band extends one standard deviation on each
              side of the mean: <m>100 - 12 = 88</m> and
              <m>100 + 12 = 112</m>.
            </p>
          </feedback>
        </choice>
        <choice>
          <statement><p>From 0 to 12</p></statement>
          <feedback>
            <p>
              This treats the standard deviation as a range of data
              values. It is a distance, and the band is built by
              stepping that distance outward from the mean.
            </p>
          </feedback>
        </choice>
        <choice>
          <statement><p>From 100 to 112</p></statement>
          <feedback>
            <p>
              This steps only to the right. The typical range is
              centered at the mean and extends in both directions.
            </p>
          </feedback>
        </choice>
        <choice>
          <statement><p>From 76 to 124</p></statement>
          <feedback>
            <p>
              This steps two standard deviations each way. That is a
              useful interval, and one we will meet later, but it is not
              the typical range described here.
            </p>
          </feedback>
        </choice>
      </choices>
    </exercise>
  </subsection>

  <subsection xml:id="subsec-comparing-with-sd">
    <title>Comparing two groups</title>

    <p>
      The standard deviation earns its keep in comparisons. A school
      weighed the backpacks of 25 younger students and 25 older
      students. <xref ref="fig-backpack-younger"/> and
      <xref ref="fig-backpack-older"/> show the results, drawn with
      identical bin widths and identical axes so the two can be compared
      directly.
    </p>

    <figure xml:id="fig-backpack-younger">
      <caption>Backpack weights for 25 younger students, with the mean
      and the standard deviation band marked.</caption>
      <image xml:id="image-backpack-younger"
             source="R-generated/backpack-younger" width="90%">
        <description>
          A histogram of backpack weights for 25 younger students, with
          bars two pounds wide on an axis running from 0 to 32 pounds.
          The bars are 5 students for 2 to 4 pounds, 6 for 4 to 6, 8 for
          6 to 8, and 6 for 8 to 10. Every other bin is empty, so the
          whole distribution occupies a single tight mound between 2 and
          10 pounds at the far left of the axis. A dashed vertical line
          marks the mean at 5.8 pounds, and a shaded band about 2.1
          pounds wide on each side covers roughly 3.7 to 7.9 pounds,
          containing 14 of the 25 weights.
        </description>
      </image>
    </figure>

    <figure xml:id="fig-backpack-older">
      <caption>Backpack weights for 25 older students, on the same
      axes.</caption>
      <image xml:id="image-backpack-older"
             source="R-generated/backpack-older" width="90%">
        <description>
          A histogram of backpack weights for 25 older students, on the
          same axis from 0 to 32 pounds with the same two-pound bars.
          The bars are 3, 2, 2, 3, 2, 3, 2, 3, 2, and 1 across the bins
          running from 4 up to 24 pounds, a broad and nearly flat
          spread with no clear peak. The bins from 24 to 30 pounds are
          empty, and then a bar of 2 students appears for 30 to 32
          pounds, separated from the rest by a gap of eight pounds. A
          dashed vertical line marks the mean at 14.2 pounds, and a
          shaded band about 7.2 pounds wide on each side covers roughly
          7.0 to 21.4 pounds, containing 18 of the 25 weights. Both the
          overall spread and the shaded band are far wider than for the
          younger students.
        </description>
      </image>
    </figure>

    <example xml:id="ex-backpack-comparison">
      <title>Describing the two groups</title>
      <statement>
        <p>
          Compare the backpack weights of the two groups using the mean
          and the standard deviation.
        </p>
      </statement>
      <solution>
        <p>
          The younger students carry a mean of 5.8 pounds with a
          standard deviation of 2.1 pounds. The older students carry a
          mean of 14.2 pounds with a standard deviation of 7.2 pounds.
        </p>
        <p>
          The centers differ: the older students carry about two and a
          half times as much weight on average. But the two groups
          differ in a second way that the means alone do not reveal. The
          younger group is highly consistent. Its typical range runs
          from about 3.7 to about 7.9 pounds, a band only 4.2 pounds
          wide, and every weight in the group falls between 2 and 9
          pounds. Knowing a younger student's backpack weight is almost
          predictable from the mean alone.
        </p>
        <p>
          The older group is not. Its typical range runs from about 7.0
          to about 21.4 pounds, a band more than three times as wide,
          and the histogram is nearly flat from 4 to 24 pounds with no
          clear peak. Two students carry 30 and 31 pounds, separated
          from everyone else by an eight-pound gap. For this group the
          mean of 14.2 is a poor prediction of any individual weight.
        </p>
        <p>
          The lesson is that the two groups differ in center
          <em>and</em> in consistency, and it takes both numbers to say
          so.
        </p>
      </solution>
    </example>

    <p>
      Those two heavy packs are also a warning. Like the mean, and for
      the same structural reason, the standard deviation is not
      <term>resistant</term>: every observation enters the calculation,
      and squaring means a distant one enters with enormous weight. We
      have already seen how severe this can be. In
      <xref ref="sec-choosing-center"/> a student had 26 homework
      scores, 25 of them clustered in the 80s and one recorded as 0.
      That single score raises the standard deviation from 5.8 to 17.6
      <mdash/> it triples it. One observation out of twenty-six does
      that, because a deviation of about 82 becomes about 6,700 once
      squared.
    </p>

    <exercise xml:id="check-sd-backpack">
      <statement>
        <p>
          Suppose a third group of students has a mean backpack weight
          of 14.2 pounds, the same as the older group, but a standard
          deviation of 2.0 pounds. What does this tell you?
        </p>
      </statement>
      <choices randomize="yes">
        <choice correct="yes">
          <statement>
            <p>This group carries about as much weight on average, but
            much more consistently.</p>
          </statement>
          <feedback>
            <p>
              Correct. The equal means say the centers agree; the much
              smaller standard deviation says the weights huddle close
              to that center instead of sprawling from 4 to 31 pounds.
            </p>
          </feedback>
        </choice>
        <choice>
          <statement>
            <p>This group carries less weight overall.</p>
          </statement>
          <feedback>
            <p>
              The means are equal, so the groups carry the same weight
              on average. The standard deviation describes consistency,
              not amount.
            </p>
          </feedback>
        </choice>
        <choice>
          <statement>
            <p>This group must contain fewer students.</p>
          </statement>
          <feedback>
            <p>
              Sample size does not determine the standard deviation. A
              group of any size can be consistent or scattered.
            </p>
          </feedback>
        </choice>
        <choice>
          <statement>
            <p>A mistake has been made, since two groups with the same
            mean must have the same standard deviation.</p>
          </statement>
          <feedback>
            <p>
              They need not. The mean locates the center and the
              standard deviation measures spread about it; the next
              subsection is built entirely on separating the two.
            </p>
          </feedback>
        </choice>
      </choices>
    </exercise>
  </subsection>

  <subsection xml:id="subsec-mean-and-sd-independent">
    <title>Mean and standard deviation measure different things</title>

    <p>
      The backpack groups differed in both center and spread at once,
      which makes it easy to blur the two. Stripping away the difference
      in center shows that the standard deviation carries information
      the mean cannot.
    </p>

    <p>
      Data sets C and D below have the same mean of 50 and, as it
      happens, the same median of 50 as well. They are not remotely the
      same distribution.
    </p>

    <figure xml:id="fig-sd-tight">
      <caption>Data set C: mean 50, standard deviation about
      3.5.</caption>
      <image xml:id="image-sd-tight" source="R-generated/sd-tight"
             width="85%">
        <description>
          A dotplot of twelve values on an axis running from 20 to 80.
          The values run from 44 to 56 and are packed closely together
          around the middle of the axis, with two dots stacked at 50. A
          dashed vertical line marks the mean at 50, and a narrow shaded
          band about 3.5 units wide on each side covers roughly 46.5 to
          53.5, containing 8 of the 12 values. Most of the axis is
          empty.
        </description>
      </image>
    </figure>

    <figure xml:id="fig-sd-spread">
      <caption>Data set D: the same mean of 50, but a standard deviation
      of about 15.8.</caption>
      <image xml:id="image-sd-spread" source="R-generated/sd-spread"
             width="85%">
        <description>
          A dotplot of twelve values on the same axis from 20 to 80. The
          values run from 25 to 75, spaced evenly five units apart
          across almost the whole axis, with two dots stacked at 50. A
          dashed vertical line marks the mean at 50, in the same place
          as for data set C, and a wide shaded band about 15.8 units
          wide on each side covers roughly 34.2 to 65.8, also containing
          8 of the 12 values. The band is more than four times as wide
          as the one for data set C.
        </description>
      </image>
    </figure>

    <p>
      The two dashed lines sit in exactly the same place. Every summary
      of center we have built would report these distributions as
      identical. Yet a typical value in C sits 3.5 units from the
      center, while a typical value in D sits 15.8 units away
      <mdash/> more than four times as far.
    </p>

    <p>
      Both bands happen to capture 8 of the 12 values, which is worth
      dwelling on. The fraction of data inside the band is similar; what
      differs is how wide the band must be to capture it. That width is
      the whole content of the standard deviation. Reporting a mean
      without a measure of spread tells a reader where the distribution
      sits and nothing whatsoever about how tightly it sits there.
    </p>

    <exercise xml:id="check-sd-same-mean">
      <statement>
        <p>
          Two samples have the same mean. What can you conclude about
          their standard deviations?
        </p>
      </statement>
      <choices randomize="yes">
        <choice correct="yes">
          <statement>
            <p>Nothing. The standard deviations may be equal, or either
            one may be larger.</p>
          </statement>
          <feedback>
            <p>
              Correct. The mean locates the center and says nothing
              about spread about it. Data sets C and D share a mean and
              a median while differing in standard deviation by a factor
              of more than four.
            </p>
          </feedback>
        </choice>
        <choice>
          <statement>
            <p>The standard deviations must also be equal.</p>
          </statement>
          <feedback>
            <p>
              Data sets C and D are a direct counterexample: identical
              means of 50, standard deviations of about 3.5 and about
              15.8.
            </p>
          </feedback>
        </choice>
        <choice>
          <statement>
            <p>The sample with more values must have the larger standard
            deviation.</p>
          </statement>
          <feedback>
            <p>
              Sample size does not drive the standard deviation. A large
              sample of nearly identical values has a small one; a tiny
              sample of wildly different values has a large one.
            </p>
          </feedback>
        </choice>
        <choice>
          <statement>
            <p>Both standard deviations must be smaller than the
            mean.</p>
          </statement>
          <feedback>
            <p>
              No such relationship holds. A distribution with a small
              mean and a few very large values can easily have a
              standard deviation exceeding its mean.
            </p>
          </feedback>
        </choice>
      </choices>
    </exercise>

    <exercise xml:id="check-sd-interpret">
      <statement>
        <p>
          Two machines fill bottles labeled 500 milliliters. Machine A
          delivers a mean of 500 ml with a standard deviation of 2 ml;
          machine B delivers a mean of 500 ml with a standard deviation
          of 20 ml. In two or three sentences, explain which machine you
          would rather use and why, using the typical range of values
          for each. Then state one thing these two standard deviations
          do <em>not</em> tell you.
        </p>
        <response/>
      </statement>
      <solution>
        <p>
          Machine A is clearly preferable. Both machines are correct on
          average, so neither systematically overfills or underfills,
          but a typical bottle from machine A falls between 498 and 502
          ml, while a typical bottle from machine B falls between 480
          and 520 ml. A customer buying a single bottle from machine B
          could easily receive four percent less than the label
          promises, and the equal means offer no protection against
          that, because overfilled and underfilled bottles cancel in the
          average.
        </p>
        <p>
          The standard deviations say nothing about the shape of either
          distribution, so machine B's errors could be spread smoothly
          or could come from occasional wild misfills among otherwise
          accurate bottles. They also say nothing about any individual
          bottle, and nothing about whether a few extreme results are
          inflating machine B's value, since the standard deviation is
          not resistant.
        </p>
        <p>
          An adequate answer chooses machine A, notes that the means are
          equal, and builds at least one typical range from a mean and a
          standard deviation. A strong answer also names something the
          standard deviation cannot reveal <mdash/> shape, individual
          values, or the influence of a few extreme observations.
        </p>
      </solution>
    </exercise>
  </subsection>

  <conclusion xml:id="summary-standard-deviation">
    <p>
      The standard deviation is built exactly like the average deviation
      from the mean, with one step changed: the deviations are squared
      rather than made positive by absolute value. Squaring gives values
      far from the mean disproportionate weight, which is both the
      measure's chief strength and the source of its sensitivity to
      extremes.
    </p>
    <p>
      Three steps complete the calculation. Add the squared deviations;
      divide by <m>n - 1</m> to obtain the variance <m>s^2</m>; take the
      square root to return to the units of the data. In symbols,
      <m>s = \sqrt{\sum (x - \bar{x})^2 \, / \, (n - 1)}</m>. The
      standard deviation carries the same units as the data, is never
      negative, and equals zero exactly when every value is identical.
    </p>
    <p>
      Marking one standard deviation on each side of the mean gives a
      typical range of values, read exactly as the ADM band was. The
      standard deviation of a data set is generally somewhat larger than
      its ADM, and the gap grows as the tails of the distribution grow
      more extreme.
    </p>
    <p>
      The mean and the standard deviation answer independent questions.
      Two distributions can share a mean, and even a median, while
      differing in standard deviation by a factor of four or more.
      Reporting a center without a spread describes only half of a
      distribution.
    </p>
    <p>
      Like the mean, the standard deviation is not resistant. A single
      distant observation can multiply it several times over, which
      raises the question of when to report it at all <mdash/> the
      subject of the next section.
    </p>
    <p>
      Adapted from <em>Concepts in Statistics</em> (SUNY / Lumen
      Learning), CC BY 4.0. The backpack weights and the two same-mean
      data sets are reconstructed to reproduce the means and standard
      deviations published in the source; the underlying observations
      are not supplied there.
    </p>
  </conclusion>
</section>
